Looking at this problem:

- `n ≤ 7`, so at most 14 elements → bitmask DP over `2^14 = 16384` states is feasible.
- Key insight: although operations are scored by their index, we can process pairs in any order we *choose* — if `k` elements are already used (mask popcount), the next operation index is `k/2 + 1`.
- To avoid counting pair permutations multiple times, always pair the lowest-indexed unused element with each other unused element.

**DP definition:** `dp[mask]` = maximum score achievable where `mask` is the set of already-used elements. Start with `dp[0] = 0`, and for each mask, extend by pairing the first unused element `i` with every other unused element `j`, adding `op * gcd(nums[i], nums[j])` where `op = popcount(mask)/2 + 1`. Answer is `dp[full]`.

**Verification with Example 2** (`[3,4,6,8]`): from empty mask, pairing (3,6) gives dp=3 at op 1; then pairing (4,8) at op 2 adds 8 → 11. ✓

**Complexity:** `O(2^(2n) · n)` time ≈ 230k operations, `O(2^(2n))` space. Max possible score ≈ 7 · 7 · 10⁶ fits in `int`.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <numeric>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int maxScore(vector<int>& nums) {
        int m = nums.size();
        int full = (1 << m) - 1;
        vector<int> dp(full + 1, 0);

        for (int mask = 0; mask < full; mask++) {
            int c = __builtin_popcount(mask);
            if (c & 1) continue;  // only even counts of used elements are reachable
            int op = c / 2 + 1;   // next operation index (1-indexed)

            int i = __builtin_ctz(~mask);  // first unused element
            for (int j = i + 1; j < m; j++) {
                if (mask & (1 << j)) continue;
                int nmask = mask | (1 << i) | (1 << j);
                int gain = op * std::gcd(nums[i], nums[j]);
                dp[nmask] = max(dp[nmask], dp[mask] + gain);
            }
        }

        return dp[full];
    }
};
```

