This is a classic binary search on the answer problem.

The approach:
1. If `m * k > n`, it's impossible, return -1. Note: m can be up to 10^6 and k up to n (10^5), so m*k could be up to 10^11 which overflows int. Need to use long long.
2. Binary search on the number of days. The minimum day is min(bloomDay), maximum is max(bloomDay).
3. For a given day `d`, check if we can make `m` bouquets: iterate through the array, count consecutive bloomed flowers (bloomDay[i] <= d). When consecutive count reaches k, increment bouquet count and reset consecutive count. If bouquet count >= m, return true.

The check function is O(n), and binary search is O(log(max_day)), so total is O(n log(max_day)) which is fine for n = 10^5.

Let me write the code:

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int minDays(vector<int>& bloomDay, int m, int k) {
        long long need = (long long)m * k;
        int n = bloomDay.size();
        if (need > n) return -1;

        int lo = *min_element(bloomDay.begin(), bloomDay.end());
        int hi = *max_element(bloomDay.begin(), bloomDay.end());

        auto canMake = [&](int day) -> bool {
            int bouquets = 0;
            int consecutive = 0;
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                if (bloomDay[i] <= day) {
                    consecutive++;
                    if (consecutive == k) {
                        bouquets++;
                        consecutive = 0;
                        if (bouquets >= m) return true;
                    }
                } else {
                    consecutive = 0;
                }
            }
            return bouquets >= m;
        };

        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (canMake(mid)) {
                hi = mid;
            } else {
                lo = mid + 1;
            }
        }
        return lo;
    }
};
```

