Looking at this problem:

**Key insight:** Each of my picks removes my slice plus both neighbors (taken by Alice and Bob). So across the whole game, the `n` slices I pick must be **pairwise non-adjacent in the circular array** of `3n` slices. Conversely, any independent set of size `n` in the cycle is achievable (there's always a pickable slice whose removal keeps the rest independent — a gap argument shows a "safe" slice always exists since the `n` gaps between chosen slices sum to `2n`, so some gap ≥ 2 exists).

So the problem reduces to: **pick exactly `n` non-adjacent elements from a circular array maximizing sum.**

**Circular → linear:** Since elements `0` and `m-1` are adjacent in the circle, the optimal set either excludes the last element or excludes the first. Thus:
- answer = max( solveLinear(slices[0..m-2], n), solveLinear(slices[1..m-1], n) )

Both linear results correspond to valid circular independent sets, and the circular optimum is covered by one of the cases.

**Linear DP:** `dp[i][j]` = max sum picking exactly `j` non-adjacent elements from first `i`:
`dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i-2][j-1] + b[i-1])`, with the `i==1` base case allowing taking `b[0]` when `j==1`.

Complexity: O(m·n) ≈ 500 × 167 per pass — trivial.

Verified against both examples: `[1,2,3,4,5,6]` → max(8, 10) = 10 ✓; `[8,9,8,6,1,1]` → max(16, 15) = 16 ✓.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int maxSizeSlices(vector<int>& slices) {
        int m = slices.size();
        int n = m / 3;
        const int NEG = -1000000000;

        // Max sum picking exactly n non-adjacent elements from linear array b
        auto solveLinear = [&](const vector<int>& b) -> int {
            int L = b.size();
            // dp[i][j] = max sum picking exactly j non-adjacent slices from first i elements
            vector<vector<int>> dp(L + 1, vector<int>(n + 1, NEG));
            for (int i = 0; i <= L; ++i) dp[i][0] = 0;
            for (int i = 1; i <= L; ++i) {
                for (int j = 1; j <= n; ++j) {
                    int best = dp[i - 1][j]; // skip element i-1
                    int prev = (i >= 2) ? dp[i - 2][j - 1] : (j == 1 ? 0 : NEG);
                    if (prev != NEG && prev + b[i - 1] > best)
                        best = prev + b[i - 1]; // take element i-1
                    dp[i][j] = best;
                }
            }
            return dp[L][n];
        };

        // Circular array: optimal set excludes slices[m-1] or slices[0]
        vector<int> dropLast(slices.begin(), slices.end() - 1);
        vector<int> dropFirst(slices.begin() + 1, slices.end());
        return max(solveLinear(dropLast), solveLinear(dropFirst));
    }
};
```

