Looking at this problem:

- Node values are bounded (1..50), which is the key constraint to exploit.
- Do a DFS from root. Maintain, for each value `v` (1..50), a stack of currently-active nodes (on the root-to-current path) having that value. Push a node on entry, pop on exit — this guarantees each stack contains exactly the ancestors of the current node.
- When entering a node with value `v`, check the top of the stack for every value `w` coprime to `v` (precomputed lists), and pick the candidate with the greatest depth. That's the closest coprime ancestor.
- Use an iterative DFS (enter/exit events) to avoid recursion-depth issues with n up to 1e5.

