The classic wildcard matching problem. Let me think about the approach.

Standard DP approach: dp[i][j] = whether s[0..i) matches p[0..j).

Base: dp[0][0] = true. dp[0][j] = dp[0][j-1] if p[j-1] == '*', else false.

Transition:
- If p[j-1] == '*': dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-1] || dp[i-1][j] (empty match or consume one char).
- If p[j-1] == '?' or p[j-1] == s[i-1]: dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1].
- Else false.

Time O(m*n), space can be optimized to O(n) with 1D DP.

With m, n up to 2000, O(m*n) = 4,000,000 operations — fine.

Alternatively, the greedy two-pointer approach is O(m*n) worst case but usually faster. Let me use the 1D DP for simplicity and correctness.

Let me write the 1D DP:

```cpp
